INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment#2', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment', 2, 'One-paragraph read',
  '## One-paragraph read

OpenAI''s official Cookbook page describes Codex Goals as persistent, thread-scoped completion contracts for tasks where the path is uncertain but the finish line can be verified. This is a strong fit for Techscope and Agents Mother because it converts vague continuation into an auditable loop: define outcome, evidence, constraints, boundaries, iteration policy and blocked stop condition, then let Codex continue only while the Goal remains active and evidence says the task is not complete.', '93b3b9d03f05c1ea2789bc8043c6fbec1ba3271e5e4aa7529759555657c5b976', 104
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## One-paragraph read

OpenAI''s official Cookbook page describes Codex Goals as persistent, thread-scoped completion contracts for tasks where the path is uncertain but the finish line can be verified. This is a strong fit for Techscope and Agents Mother because it converts vague continuation into an auditable loop: define outcome, evidence, constraints, boundaries, iteration policy and blocked stop condition, then let Codex continue only while the Goal remains active and evidence says the task is not complete.', 'One-paragraph read', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment#2');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment#3', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment', 3, 'Why it matters',
  '## Why it matters

- Techscope already runs long tasks: building agents, processing media queues, researching architectures, checking official docs and writing standards.
- These tasks often need continuation across turns without repeatedly asking "continue".
- Goals formalize the missing harness primitive: durable objective plus evidence-based completion.
- Goals should reduce premature "done" claims if used with concrete tests, reports, command outputs or artifacts.', 'cab690691a60f8169c79829115a7728b916562c0682de829b3f6df8ac9255549', 87
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Why it matters

- Techscope already runs long tasks: building agents, processing media queues, researching architectures, checking official docs and writing standards.
- These tasks often need continuation across turns without repeatedly asking "continue".
- Goals formalize the missing harness primitive: durable objective plus evidence-based completion.
- Goals should reduce premature "done" claims if used with concrete tests, reports, command outputs or artifacts.', 'Why it matters', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment#3');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment#4', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment', 4, 'Technical claims',
  '## Technical claims

- Goals are persistent objectives in Codex that keep a thread working toward a defined outcome across turns.
- Goals are for tasks where the next step depends on what Codex learns: profiling, patching, benchmarking, flaky tests or evidence-backed research.
- Goals are thread-scoped state, not global memory and not project-level instructions.
- Continuation is conservative: Codex continues only at safe boundaries when the thread is idle, the Goal is active and no user input/work is pending.
- Completion must be evidence-based, not model belief.
- Good Goals include outcome, verification surface, constraints, boundaries, iteration policy and blocked stop condition.
- According to the source, Goals require a Codex build that supports the feature and are available starting in Codex 0.128.0.', '5c71e6e0822be9ec955d3daa83904153cd96dcb46a733b08d101c597a85b743a', 168
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Technical claims

- Goals are persistent objectives in Codex that keep a thread working toward a defined outcome across turns.
- Goals are for tasks where the next step depends on what Codex learns: profiling, patching, benchmarking, flaky tests or evidence-backed research.
- Goals are thread-scoped state, not global memory and not project-level instructions.
- Continuation is conservative: Codex continues only at safe boundaries when the thread is idle, the Goal is active and no user input/work is pending.
- Completion must be evidence-based, not model belief.
- Good Goals include outcome, verification surface, constraints, boundaries, iteration policy and blocked stop condition.
- According to the source, Goals require a Codex build that supports the feature and are available starting in Codex 0.128.0.', 'Technical claims', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment#4');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment#5', '2026-05-21-openai-codex-goals-assessment', 5, 'Agent environment profile',
  '## Agent environment profile

- Agent platforms: Codex.
- Model context: Codex app/CLI builds with Goals support.
- Runtime environment: Codex thread, Codex desktop/app and possibly CLI depending feature availability.
- Config surfaces: `/goal` command, thread state, AGENTS.md, workflows.
- Portability: codex-native.
- Codex adaptation: use Goals as an execution-control layer for long Codex tasks; keep durable project rules in AGENTS.md and standards.
- Environment-specific caveats: do not assume Claude Code, Gemini CLI or Cursor have equivalent goal semantics.', '48f1c1db84c5ddd79f0a3ceadccd286683645f5535f4050b12b7ddd8e6202e2f', 107
);
